{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Prototype code is meant to be thrown away, and the goal is to learn quickly whether an idea is worth pursuing, so checking it by whether it works is enough. Run it only in a sandbox with fake data and no real credentials, and don't let it turn into the MVP without a rewrite or a full review."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo is still mostly disposable, but people make funding and product decisions based on it, so it has to really do what it seems to do and not rely on hidden shortcuts or hardcoded results. Skim the core logic, test the demo flows, and review closely anything that touches real data, secrets, or external services."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users bring real data, so you need tests on the core flows and line-by-line review (level 2) for security-sensitive areas like authentication, authorization, payments, data handling, and data migrations. Everything else can be reviewed selectively so the team keeps moving fast, as long as someone on the team understands the overall architecture."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "A team has to maintain this code for years, and code nobody has read builds up as hidden maintenance and security debt. Review every change as carefully as you would a human colleague's pull request; level 3 is fine for clearly low-risk areas such as internal tools, test scaffolding, or well-tested boilerplate."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Regulated and safety-critical systems need people who fully understand and can vouch for every line, along with traceability and audit trails, so AI should help with suggestions, analysis, and test generation but a person should write the final code. Level 2 may be acceptable for non-critical supporting code if your compliance framework allows it, and some highly certified components may require level 0."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Match how much you trust unread AI code to how much it would cost if that code were wrong and how long it will live. Let AI write freely for code you'll throw away, rely more on tests as real users and data arrive, and require people to understand every line once the code is maintained long-term or failures could cause serious harm. In every stage, apply the stricter level to high-risk areas (security, payments, personal data, migrations), and never let code move to a later stage without being brought up to that stage's review standard."
}

